tonight I am speaking to you because  there is a growing humanitarian and  security crisis at our southern border  why should Congress cough up the money  to build a border wall one of trumps  claims is that it would help stop the  flow of deadly drugs coming in from  Mexico this is a humanitarian crisis a  crisis of the heart and a crisis of the  soul in fact marijuana has always been  the most smuggled drug from Mexico by a  longshot but over the last few years  even shipments of weed have declined by  more than 75% and this trend has nothing  to do with increased border security or  a crackdown on the Sinaloa cartel and  its former leader Joaquin el chapo  guzman who was sentenced to life in  prison in u.s. federal court last week  the fall-off in pot smuggling is a  direct result of legalization I sat down  with David bier a policy analyst at the  Cato Institute who wrote the cover story  of our April issue about how easing pot  prohibition is doing what the failed war  on drugs never could well marijuana  legalization in the United States which  really started with Colorado in  Washington and 2014 has really shifted  the supply of drugs from Mexico to state  legal growers in the United States and  that shift has reduced marijuana  smuggling dramatically along the border  in 2018 the vast majority of drugs by  value came into the country through  ports of entry not way out in the  wilderness somewhere and if you look at  it as a result of the legalization of  marijuana the overall value of drugs  crossing between those ports of entry  has declined by 70%  so the president is not only focusing on  a small share of the border drug trade  it's focused on a shrinking share of  that drug trade what a border wall have  any effect on stopping the flow of drugs  into the country well the best evidence  we have for that is between 2003 and  2009 the border patrol built over 500  miles of fencing along the border and  that had no effect whatsoever on the  drug traffic coming into the United  States and it  to be measured both by the use rates in  the United States or by the rate at  which were seizing the drugs not only  did they build these fences but they  launched a drone fleet they doubled  their workforce it had no effect and in  2013 the Drug Enforcement Administration  issued a report on drug smuggling and  they basically threw up their hands and  said we just need a lot more resources  to get a handle on drug smuggling  between ports of entry and then in five  short years almost you know 78 percent  of that drug trade between those ports  of entry for marijuana has declined and  yet we just had the longest government  shutdown in American history over the  question of whether to build a wall and  drugs were regularly raised as  justification for building that wall do  you think that there's a failure of  emphasis that the emphasis should be at  ports of entry or is there an overall  decline even across you know the entire  border at the end of the day I don't  think that the drug war at the border is  any more effective than the drug war  inside of the United States has been it  hasn't reduced demand for drugs it's  created huge problems both with our  relationship with Mexico and other  Central American and the Latin American  countries and so I don't advise him to  say we should just double down on the  drug war at ports of entry but if his  goal is to seize as many drugs as  possible have added at ports of entry  because between ports of entry taking  people's private property to build a  border wall I mean it's not even related  to drug trafficking it's so you know it  distant from the phenomenon so at this  point I would advise the president to  move on look at marijuana legalization  look at how effective that is apply some  lessons to other drugs don't double down  on the border security drug war what  about the cartels how are they  responding to legalization  well they've trying to shift to other  drugs and traffic 