r and with the immigration reduction  do you think that's true it is  absolutely the case that we have  empowered cartels along the border both  through the drug war and immigration  enforcement by narrowing the options for  legal entry you really empower cartels  who are trying to control the trade if  there's only a few entry points that  they can control then that really gives  them the Opera  to monopolize and drug wars gang wars in  Mexico are driven by the demand and  desire of those drug gangs to control  those ports of entry those those limited  areas along the border where people can  get in if Goods and people flow freely  the the control of the cartels goes down  dramatically they aren't able to charge  smuggling fees they aren't able to  control the ports of entry and you won't  have the problems that we see and the  control that we see by the drug cartels  right now would you say that the cartels  respond to supply and demand in the  American market and how easy it is to  bring it in whatever the market demands  the cartels will try to fulfill and if  you look at it from the mexican growers  perspective if the demand for marijuana  goes down it really forces them into  position of deciding is it valuable  enough to continue to grow it and  there's some very interesting anecdotes  about farmers in Mexico who are saying  just that you know in 2014 as  legalization is crashing the Mexican  marijuana market the you know they're  debating whether they can even continue  to grow marijuana what's happened to  black markets for drugs in states that  have legalized well we have a spectrum  Colorado has probably had the most  successful model for marijuana  legalization according to their  government they've essentially  eliminated the entire black market in  Colorado all that's now you know  homegrown marijuana California has been  a lot less successful in part because of  the regulations and taxes licensing laws  in particular have been an obstacle to  getting 